      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-photo-wrap"><img class="bio-photo" src="/assets/past-bios/gnowarre/lisa-rawson.jpg" alt="Lisa Rawson" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Lisa Rawson</h2><div class="bio-text prose"><p>is the playwright of &quot;Lost, Then Found In The Middle Of Gnowarre&quot; which is her second comedy. She's a PACA rookie, but has performed in various Erie theatres for many years. Believing that we all need reasons to laugh more, she hopes you enjoy this comedic clash of the cultures. GO GNOMES!</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Monica William</h2><div class="bio-text prose"><p>Spent several years in New York City performing stand-up and improv. She is a long standing member of In All Seriousness, Erie's own comedy-improv group. She has performed all over he tri-state area in murder mysteries with the IAS-Murder Division. She has also appeared in many productions at the Station Dinner Theater including a few productions of A Canterbury Feast. And she has especially enjoyed participating in a Attack of the PACA a few times as well.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-photo-wrap"><img class="bio-photo" src="/assets/past-bios/gnowarre/renee-tellers.jpg" alt="Renee Tellers" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Renee Tellers</h2><div class="bio-text prose"><p>Renée is happy to be back on the PACA stage. When she is not acting she is the co-host of the history podcast, Time Tellers and the violinist for the band Comic Book Villains. Past performances include POTUS, Bad Actors Bad Auditions, and various Playhouse shows both on stage and in the orchestra pit.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-photo-wrap"><img class="bio-photo" src="/assets/past-bios/gnowarre/richard-j-kress.jpg" alt="Richard J. Kress" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Richard J. Kress</h2><p class="bio-role">Steve</p><div class="bio-text prose"><p>Richard appears daily in the lifelong roles of husband to his wife Holly and father to his three exceptional children. The entire Kress family can be found on stages and behind the scenes all around the region. They reside in Waterford, PA and Richard works in Erie at Kress Financial Services.<br>
Richard has performed at PACA in Closer Than Ever and last year's Holiday Extravaganza as a soloist and with all 5 &quot;Von Kress family singers&quot; which was a very special experience. At the Station Dinner Theatre: John/Suzanne, NED Sullivan, and Steve Sullivan (in the Erie premier of Gnowarre). He often travels to Academy in Meadville and has portrayed: Sam Carmichael, Mister Sowerberry, Captain Von Trapp, Inspector Kemp, Victor Von Frankenstein, &amp; Eugene Beauregarde, Agustín Magaldi. Favorite Erie Playhouse roles include Prince Topher and Bob Wallace. At Barrow: Topher (again). At Gannon: Herbie. Richard also sails and sings aboard the Victorian Princess for Italian dinner cruises with smooth crooner standards.<br>
Richard has a growing list of technical credits for sound, light, projection, and effects at Footlights, Academy, Barrow, Station Dinner Theatre, PACA, Penny &amp; Pearl Productions, Behrend, and Gannon. <br>
He also directs student productions at Robison Elementary.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-photo-wrap"><img class="bio-photo" src="/assets/past-bios/gnowarre/vincent-murphy.jpg" alt="Vincent Murphy" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Vincent Murphy</h2><div class="bio-text prose"><p>is a relatively new addition to both Erie and it's acting scene. He was recently seen bringing fear to Eeriebyss Factory of Terror as a fill in for William Afton in the Five Nights at Freddy's section. However he really found his stride as a comical frightening version of Dr Frankenstein. He wishes to reiterate that he is a fierce Bills fan, but really a happy soccer wanker. He thanks his friends and family for their continued love and support, especially his mother Joy, father Kraig, and annoying siblings Demetri and Lucy. G-d bless, and you'll never walk alone.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-photo-wrap"><img class="bio-photo" src="/assets/past-bios/gnowarre/jeff-rodland.jpg" alt="Jeff Rodland" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Jeff Rodland</h2><p class="bio-role">Artistic Director</p><div class="bio-text prose"><p>Jeff is the Artistic Director of PACA, and has produced, directed and has been involved in hundreds of performances of over the past 40 years.</p></div></div>
      </div>

      <div class="bio-card">
        <div class="bio-photo-wrap"><img class="bio-photo" src="/assets/past-bios/gnowarre/mark-tanenbaum.jpg" alt="Mark Tanenbaum" loading="lazy"></div>
        <div class="bio-body"><h2 class="bio-name">Mark Tanenbaum</h2><p class="bio-role">Executive Director</p><div class="bio-text prose"><p>Before there was a PACA, there was a kid from Erie who fell in love with the stage.</p>
<p>Mark Tanenbaum has been part of Erie’s arts scene since he first laced up his dance shoes with the Erie Civic Ballet Company. From there, he danced with the Chautauqua Dance Company, performed on every major stage in the market, and logged over 1,000 professional productions — including a long run with the comedy troupe In All Seriousness.</p>
<p>In 2010, he turned a historic 1899 building on State Street into something Erie had never seen: a true home for independent, progressive performing arts. PACA was born.</p>
<p>Since then, PACA has become more than a theater. It’s a gallery. A comedy club. A dance studio. A community. A place where Erie’s artists — from first-time performers to MFA graduates — have a stage to call their own.</p>
<p>In 2020, Preservation Erie recognized PACA with a Preservation Excellence Award for breathing new life into downtown. And in 2025, the curtain rose on PACA’s 100th production.</p>
<p>Mark is proof that one person’s vision — backed by grit, a deep love for this city, and more than a few curtain calls — can change a community.</p></div></div>
      </div>

